From: thingino <thingino@opensensor>
Subject: [PATCH] mips/xburst/soc-t23/isvp/Pike: pull up the soft-I2C1 GPIO pins

Cinnado B6 T23ZN/TM18NA live-hardware bring-up: after fixing the
GPIO_I2C1_SDA/SCK pin numbers (see the preceding 0092 patch), i2cdetect
on the bitbanged bus found one consistent, repeatable device (0x10) but
also a different set of spurious extra addresses on every single scan
(0x16, then 0x36/0x37/0x3f/0x50, then 0x19/0x25/0x27/0x39/0x4f/0x54/
0x6c/0x6f/0x74 -- never the same twice) -- classic floating-line/no-
pull-up bus noise, not real devices. Software-bitbanged I2C (unlike the
hardware I2C0 controller already in use for the sensor) has no built-in
pull-up handling and needs the GPIO pins explicitly configured.

This board's Pike/gpio_customized.c already has exactly this mechanism
(a { gpio, GPIO_PULL_UP } table applied via gpio_pull_init()) but its
arch_initcall was commented out, so the whole file -- table and all --
was dead code for every T23-ISVP board. Add pull-ups for the two soft-
I2C1 pins (GPIO_PB(20)/GPIO_PB(21), i.e. GPIO52/53 -- SCL/SDA) and
enable the initcall so it actually runs. This also activates the
existing (harmless, MMC-signal-quality-improving) drive-strength table
for the TF card pins, previously dead code too.

diff --git a/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t23/chip-t23/isvp/Pike/gpio_customized.c b/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t23/chip-t23/isvp/Pike/gpio_customized.c
--- a/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t23/chip-t23/isvp/Pike/gpio_customized.c
+++ b/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t23/chip-t23/isvp/Pike/gpio_customized.c
@@ -16,6 +16,8 @@
 static gpio_pull_table_t soc_gpio_pull_table[] = {
 // Dont force the pull state of the GPIO since we may not be using it for the TF cd pin
 //	{ GPIO_PB(27), GPIO_PULL_UP }, //TF cd
+	{ GPIO_PB(20), GPIO_PULL_UP }, //I2C1 SCL (soft-gpio, MCU bus)
+	{ GPIO_PB(21), GPIO_PULL_UP }, //I2C1 SDA (soft-gpio, MCU bus)
 };

 static gpio_drive_strength_table_t soc_gpio_drive_strength_table[] = {
@@ -62,4 +64,4 @@
 	return 0;
 }

-//arch_initcall(gpio_customized_init);
+arch_initcall(gpio_customized_init);
